     "type": "intro",
     "title": "A Star Is a Controlled Bomb",
     "content": "Look up at the Sun and you are staring at a nuclear furnace that has burned steadily for **4.6 billion years** and will keep burning for about 5 billion more. How?\n\nA star is caught in a **tug-of-war**. Its own gravity is crushing it inward, trying to collapse a ball of gas the size of a million Earths down to a point. Something pushes back with exactly equal force, and that standoff is why the Sun doesn't shrink or explode.\n\nThat outward push comes from **heat** — and the heat comes from **nuclear fusion** in the core, where hydrogen is being welded into helium at 15 million degrees.\n\nIn this lesson you'll learn what really powers a star, why **E = mc²** is the source of sunlight, how four protons become one helium nucleus, and how physicists figured all this out without ever visiting a star.",
