     "explanation": "Position on the main sequence is essentially a mass ranking. Star A (hot, blue, luminous, top-left) is high-mass — its intense gravity forces a hot core that burns hydrogen wastefully fast, so it dies in a few million years. Star B (cool, red, dim, bottom-right) is a low-mass red dwarf that sips its fuel and can last trillions of years. More brightness means a faster burn rate, not a longer life.",
