     "explanation": "Cepheid variables are 'standard candles': a Cepheid's pulsation period reveals its true intrinsic brightness (Leavitt's law). Hubble timed Cepheids in Andromeda, deduced they were genuinely luminous, yet saw they appeared extremely faint. By the inverse-square law, that faintness meant an enormous distance — far beyond the ~100,000-light-year span of the Milky Way. Andromeda had to be a separate galaxy, settling the Great Debate in Curtis's favor.",
