     "type": "application",
     "title": "Your Turn: Keep Exploring",
     "content": "Here is the parting gift this whole book wants to hand you: the cosmos is open to anyone who looks up, and you are now ready to explore it for yourself. You do not need a telescope on a mountain or a physics degree. Try these concrete next steps.\n\n• **Go outside and look up.** On a clear, dark night, away from city lights, let your eyes adjust for twenty minutes and simply take in the sky. Find the Moon's craters, spot a planet, trace the faint band of the Milky Way. Everything in this book is hanging right there over your head.\n\n• **Get a small telescope or good binoculars.** Even modest binoculars reveal the moons of Jupiter, the phases of Venus, the craters of the Moon, and smudges of distant galaxies. It is the same view that rewrote human history when Galileo first turned a lens skyward.\n\n• **Use a stargazing app.** Free phone apps identify stars, planets, and constellations in real time. Just hold your phone to the sky and it names what you are seeing. It turns a field of dots into a map you can read.\n\n• **Join citizen science.** Projects like Galaxy Zoo let anyone classify real galaxies from telescope data, and other efforts invite you to help hunt for planets and asteroids. Amateurs make genuine discoveries this way.\n\n• **Read a great book or take a free course.** For plain-language wonder, try Carl Sagan's *Cosmos*, or a free online astronomy course. Pick one and let it pull you deeper.\n\nThe universe took 13.8 billion years to make something that could understand it. That something is you. Keep looking up.",
